  "articleSummary": "**Summary:**\n\n* **Core Thesis:** The primary security threat of AI agents is not a single sci-fi breakthrough, but the compounding multiplication of agent endurance (task length) and breadth (orchestrated sub-agents), amplified by \"LLMjacking\" where attackers use stolen credentials so victims pay the compute meter.\n* **Key Technologies & Concepts:** Multi-hour autonomous agents, orchestrators (e.g., Anthropic's Claude Code), METR's time-horizon benchmarks, and LLMjacking (stealing cloud access to run free automated swarms). \n* **Tone & Framing:** An analytical, pragmatic warning using the *Terminator* (\"absolutely will not stop billing\") framing device to debunk the idea that AI threat models rely on sci-fi general intelligence rather than mundane economic and compounding capability curves.\n* **Intended Audience:** Software engineering teams, security professionals, and cloud infrastructure architects skeptical of agentic threat hype.",
